536	  // The two write-backs onto the request issue (DESIGN §16.5). Whoever converges
537	  // the item owns the write-back for the end it converged: the executor for a
538	  // declined request, the session for a run that left a PR. A run that FAILED
539	  // writes nothing and leaves the queued label standing — re-arming work that
540	  // writes code is a person's decision, and that standing label is also what stops
541	  // the scheduler run adopting the same request a second time.
542	  // A refused request DISARMS on the issue itself (§16.5): the terminal
543	  // status stands on the issue — which stays whatever open/closed state its
544	  // author keeps it in, because the run's verdict is not the issue's validity —
545	  // and blocks re-adoption until a person clears it (the re-ask lever). The
546	  // one comment says why. There is no separate in-review write-back: the
547	  // approval park IS the in-review state, on the same labels.
548	  function declineRequest(it, why) {
549	    const req = requestOf(it.request);
550	    if (!req) return;
551	    req.comments.push({ login: 'claudinite', body: `declined: ${why}` });
552	    record('request-declined', { issue: req.number, why });
553	  }
